
=== Data types ===

X509_Algorithm
  This structure defines the handling of signatures and public keys based
  one one algorithm pair.

  void*userdata = Not used; you can store your own data here.

  const uint8_t*s_oid = The binary OID for the signature algorithm.

  size_t s_oidlen = Length (in bytes) of the s_oid;

  const uint8_t*k_oid = The binary OID for the public key algorithm.

  size_t k_oidlen = Length (in bytes) of the k_oid;

  int(*check_signature)(const X509_Algorithm*al, const X509_Info*info,
  const uint8_t*data, size_t len, const ASN1_Value*publickey, const
  ASN1_Value* algorithm, const ASN1_Value*signature) = Has the same meaning
  as the check_signature callback function in the X509_Options structure,
  except that the first argument is the pointer to this X509_Algorithm.

  int(*make_public_key)(conts X509_Algorithm*al, const X509_Info*info,
  void*privatekey, const ASN1_Value*keyalgorithm, ASN1_Encoder*encoder) =
  Converts a private key to a public key. If successful, it should write
  a ASN.1 bit string to the encoder and return X509_OK, otherwise it should
  return a nonzero error code.

  int(*make_signature)(const X509_Algorithm*al, const X509_Info*info, const
  uint8_t*data, size_t len, void*privatekey, const ASN1_Value*algorithm,
  ASN1_Value*signature) = Same meaning as make_signature in X509_Options,
  except that the first argument is the pointer to this X509_Algorithm.

X509_Chain
  A chain of X.509 certificates. The root certificate comes first and then
  the rest of the certificates come next, and the end certificate will be
  the last one. There are two fields:

  ASN1_Value*item = The list of certificates.

  uint16_t count = The number of certificates in this chain.

X509_Encoder
  An opaque object for making a new certificate.

X509_Extension
  This structure defines the handling of X.509 extensions.

  int(*call)(const X509_Extension*ext, X509_Info*info, const X509_Options*
  option, const ASN1_Value*data, uint8_t crit) = This function is called
  when reading the extension from a certificate. The first argument is a
  pointer to the X509_Extension structure, the second argument is a pointer
  to the info for the current certificate (not the entire chain, although
  some parts will depend on certificates in the chain that have already
  been handled), the third argument is the option pointer that was passed
  to one of the x509_read_ functions, the fourth argument is the value of
  the extension (the octet string is already decoded into a ASN.1 value),
  and the fifth argument is 1 if it is critical or 0 if not critical.

  void*userdata = Not used; you can store your own data here.

  const uint8_t*oid = Pointer to the binary OID of this extension.

  size_t oidlen = Length (in bytes) of the OID.

  uint8_t flag = Flags for handling this extension. This is the bitwise OR
  of zero or more of the flags listed below.

  X509_IGNORE_UNLESS_CRITICAL = Causes it to avoid calling the callback
  function if the extension is not critical.

  X509_REQUIRE_EXTENSION_END = Fails if this extension is not present in
  the end certificate. This is not relevant for CRL extensions.

  X509_REQUIRE_EXTENSION_CA = Fails if this extension is not present in
  any certificate that is not the end certificate. This is not relevant
  for CRL extensions.

  X509_SECOND_PHASE = If this is set, then it will add a second phase of
  handling extensions, and will use this one during the second phase.

  X509_REJECT_IF_CRITICAL = Causes the certificate to be rejected if this
  extension is critical. (It automatically does this for any extension that
  is not recognized.)

X509_ExtraData
  An opaque structure containing data associated with keys, where the keys
  are pointers. The pointers used as keys are not read or written; only the
  address is relevant. Use the "x509_extra_" functions to deal with this.

X509_Info
  Contains information about a parsed certificate. The fields are:

  ASN1_Value subject = The subject name.

  ASN1_Value issuer = The issuer name.

  ASN1_Value subject_id = The subject unique ID. If not present, the class,
  type, and length are all zero and the data is null.

  ASN1_Value issuer_id = The issuer unique ID. Like the subject ID, this
  is not necessarily present.

  ASN1_Value publickey = The subject public key.

  time_t starts = The not valid before date.

  time_t expires = The not valid after date. If a certificate chain is
  read, this is the earliest expiry date of the entire chain.

  uint16_t count = The number of certificates after this one in the chain.
  The x509_read_certificate function leaves this alone and assumes that the
  value is correct, but x509_read_chain will automatically set its value
  when reading each certificate; when it is finished, it will be zero,
  unless an error occurred.

  X509_ExtraData*in = The input extra data (passed to one of the x509_read_
  functions).

  X509_ExtraData*out = The output extra data.

  int8_t phase = Automatically set during reading the certificate, and can
  be read during extension processing to determine if it is the first phase
  (0) or the second phase (1) of processing extensions. It will be -1 if it
  is a certificate chain.

X509_Options
  Contains options for handling X.509 certificates and chains.

  void*userdata = No use; you can use it for your own use.

  const X509_Extension*extlist = List of extension handlers. These must be
  in the ascending order of the length of the OID (in bytes), and secondly
  in the sorted order of the OIDs of the same length. You should not have
  duplicate OIDs in this list.

  uint32_t extcount = Number of extension handlers. If this is zero, then
  the pointer to the list of extension handlers is ignored and may be null.

  time_t now = The time for which the certificates are validated. In order
  for the validation to be successful, they must be valid during the time
  that is mentioned here.

  uint16_t flag = Bitwise OR of zero or more of the option flags.

  See the below section for the list of callback functions that are in
  this structure.


=== Option flags ===

X509_IGNORE_NOT_VALID_BEFORE
  Causes the not valid before date to be ignored.

X509_IGNORE_NOT_VALID_AFTER
  Causes the not valid after date to be ignored.

X509_IGNORE_SIGNATURE
  Skips validating the signature. Ignored for x509_read_certificate; this
  is only relevant for x509_read_chain.

X509_NO_STANDARD_EXTENSIONS
  If not specified, then it will use the built-in extension handling for
  some extensions that this library knows how to deal with if you have not
  specified your own handlers for those extensions; the use of these will
  be affected by some of the other options.

X509_ASSUME_NOT_CA
  Disallows X.509 version 3 certificates that lack a basic constraints
  extension from issuing further certificates.

X509_IGNORE_EXTENSIONS
  Causes it to ignore unknown critical extensions. Normally, an unknown
  critical extension is an error.

X509_ROOT_LAST
  Means the root certificate comes last instead of first in the list of
  certificates given to x509_read_chain. (The root certificate is processed
  first regardless of this flag.)

X509_REVERSE_AUTHORITY
  Causes it to check authority from the end certificate to the root instead
  of from the root to the end. If this flag is set, then when trying to
  find the authority it will not fill in the full info structure, so most
  of its contents will be unreliable, except "count" and "in", both of
  which are valid.

X509_NO_SIGNATURE
  When creating a certificate, this flag causes it to only create the main
  part of the certificate, without the signature. In this case, you must
  add the signature afterward.

X509_USER1_OPTION
  No built-in use; you can use it for your own use.

X509_USER2_OPTION
  No built-in use; you can use it for your own use.


=== Error codes ===

Any of the ASN1_ error codes are also possible, in addition to these ones.

X509_OK
  Worked successfully; not an error. (The code of this is always zero, and
  the code of ASN1_OK is also always zero.)

X509_IMPROPER_TYPE
  Some value within the certificate has the wrong type. (For some cases,
  it will return X509_IMPROPER_FORMAT instead.) (X509_IMPROPER_TYPE is the
  same as ASN1_IMPROPER_TYPE.)

X509_NOT_ENOUGH_FIELDS
  A structure within the certificate does not have enough fields.
  (X509_NOT_ENOUGH_FIELDS is the same as ASN1_DONE.)

X509_ERROR
  A general error that no other code applies.

X509_EXPIRED
  The certificate is expired.

X509_NOT_VALID_YET
  The certificate is not valid yet but will be valid in future (and should
  be checked again at that time, if it is to be used at that time).

X509_NAME_MISMATCH
  There are mismatching certificates in the chain, due to the issuer name
  and subject name (or ID) not matching.

X509_IMPROPER_FORMAT
  The certificate (or a part of it) is not in the expected format.

X509_EXT_ERROR
  This error code can be used by the extension callback function to
  indicate an error with the extension. (Extension callbacks can return
  other error codes as well; they are not required to use this one.)

X509_WRONG_SIGNATURE
  The signature cannot be verified because it does not match the
  certificate with the issuer's key.

X509_UNKNOWN_SIGNATURE
  The signature cannot be verified because it uses an unknown algorithm.

X509_UNKNOWN_AUTHORITY
  Can be returned from one of the callback functions to indicate that the
  authority is unknown. If find_authority is not null, then it may be
  called again with the next certificate until it finds one which is a
  valid authority.

X509_UNKNOWN_EXTENSION
  The certificate contains an unknown critical extension.

X509_REPEATED_EXTENSION
  The certificate contains multiple extensions with the same OID.

X509_REVOKED
  The certificate should not be used because it is revoked.

X509_REJECTED_EXTENSION
  An extension is rejected, possibly because it is critical but that
  extension is not allowed to be critical.

X509_DEFER_EXTENSION
  Not inherently an error. It can be returned from the extension callback
  in order to cause it to be called again during the second phase. If this
  code is returned during the second phase, then it is treated as an error
  and is returned from x509_read_certificate or x509_read_chain.

X509_MISSING_EXTENSION
  The certificate lacks a required extension.

X509_ACCESS_DENIED
  Some permission is required that is not granted by the certificate chain
  (either implicitly or explicitly).

X509_NOT_IMPLEMENTED
  Something which is not implemented (it might be implemented in future).

X509_IMPROPER_STEP
  One of the functions for making certificates has been called at the wrong
  time. They must be called in the correct sequence; if you call them in
  the wrong sequence then it will return this error code.

X509_IMPROPER_KEY
  This error code is intended to be used by the functions to make and
  verify signatures to indicate an unusable key. None of the built-in
  functions will return this error code unless a callback function does.


=== Callback functions ===

This describes the callback functions in the X509_Options structure. Unless
otherwise specified, any of them may be null.

int(*begin_chain)(const X509_Chain*chain,const X509_Options*option,X509_ExtraData*extra)
  If this function is defined, it is called when x509_read_chain is called,
  before any other processing is done. If it returns nonzero then it will
  return immediately from x509_read_chain.

int(*check_info)(const X509_Info*info,void*userdata)
  After doing everything for one certificate other than verifying the
  signature, if it seems valid, it will call this function, and if it
  returns nonzero then it will be treated as an error.

int(*check_revoked)(const X509_Info*info,const X509_Options*option,const ASN1_Value*certificate,const ASN1_Value*serial);
  Check if a certificate is revoked. If it is revoked, it should return
  X509_REVOKED; if not, it should return X509_OK. The info is the info
  so far of the certificate; you can use the issuer name or issuer ID
  together with the serial number in order to compare it with records in
  a revocation list, or you can use the full certificate and compute a
  hash to look it up if you are using that method instead.

int(*check_signature)(const X509_Info*info,const uint8_t*data,size_t len,const ASN1_Value*publickey,const ASN1_Value*algorithm,const ASN1_Value*signature)
  Check the signature of the certificate. The public key comes from the
  issuer certificate, and the other parameters are from the subject
  certificate. It should return X509_OK if OK or an error code (usually
  X509_WRONG_SIGNATURE or X509_UNKNOWN_SIGNATURE) otherwise.

int(*end_chain)(const X509_Chain*chain,const X509_Options*option,X509_ExtraData*extra,X509_Info*info,int status)
  This function is called just before x509_read_chain will return, whether
  it is OK or an error. It is not called if begin_chain returns nonzero.
  The fifth argument (status) is the status code; if this is nonzero then
  the info might not be valid and should be ignored. The return value from
  this callback function will be the return value of x509_read_chain.

int(*find_authority)(const X509_Info*info,const ASN1_Value*certificate)
  Called for each certificate in the chain, until a valid authority is
  found. If it returns X509_OK then it will stop calling this to find a
  authority and assume that one is already found; X509_UNKNOWN_AUTHORITY
  means that this authority is not valid but there might be another one
  which can be used; any other return value is an error. This will be
  called after parsing the certificate, unless the X509_REVERSE_AUTHORITY
  flag is specified in the options.

int(*find_issuer)(const X509_Info*info,const ASN1_Value*certificate,ASN1_Value*out)
  Find the issuer of the first certificate if it is not self-signed. If it
  is found and is an acceptable certificate, then it should write it into
  the third argument and return X509_OK, otherwise it returns an error
  code. This may also remember details such as any restrictions on the use
  of that certificate which are not a part of the certificate itself. If
  this is null, then certificates that are not self-signed are rejected.
  It will call asn1_free on the output; set out->own=0 if you want to avoid
  freeing any memory in this way.

int(*find_root)(const X509_Info*info,const ASN1_Value*certificate)
  Check if a given root certificate which is self-signed is considered
  acceptable; should return X509_OK if it is or an error code if it isn't
  OK. If this is null, then self-signed certificates use find_issuer
  instead, as though it is not self-signed. If both are null, then it will
  assume that the first one is considered acceptable (but will still
  validate it). If find_root is not null but find_issuer is null, then
  only self-signed certificates will be considered acceptable. Set this to
  x509_accept_any_self_signed to accept any self-signed certificate without
  accepting any others.

int(*make_signature)(const X509_Info*info,const uint8_t*data,size_t len,void*privatekey,const ASN1_Value*algorithm,ASN1_Value*signature)
  Make up the signature of the specified data and length using the
  specified private key and algorithm. The signature should be written into
  the ASN1_Value structure specified by the last argument. It should return
  X509_OK if it is successful, or an error code (X509_UNKNOWN_SIGNATURE if
  it is not a recognized algorithm) if it is an error. It will free the
  signature value by asn1_free, so you do not need to do that yourself.

int(*mid_chain)(const X509_Chain*chain,const X509_Options*option,X509_ExtraData*extra,X509_Info*info)
  Called between certificates in a chain (not before the first one and not
  after the last one). The info will be the info for the certificate that
  has just been read, and you can use this to change the extra data between
  certificates in case the output of one needs to affect the input of the
  next one, or to copy out some of the intermediate info, or to modify the
  info that will be used to match the issuer info of the next certificate
  (and to verify the signature, in case there is somehow some reason to use
  a different public key than the one in the issuer certificate).

int(*set_serial)(const X509_Info*info,void*userdata)
  A callback function used by the certificate making functions if the
  x509_set_serial function is not called before x509_set_signature. This
  callback function must call x509_set_serial and then return X509_OK. If
  this is not defined, then it is an error to call x509_set_signature
  without first calling x509_set_serial successfully.


=== Certificate reading functions ===

int x509_read_certificate(const ASN1_Value*cert,const X509_Options*option,X509_ExtraData*extra,X509_Info*info)
  Read and validate a certificate.

  cert = The certificate to read.

  option = The options for reading and validating the certificate.

  extra = Input extra data required for validation.

  info = The structure that the information about the certificate will be
  written into. Normally, you should call x509_reset_info first (although
  doing so is not necessary if it is already zeroed).

  The return value is an error message.

int x509_read_chain(const X509_Chain*chain,const X509_Options*option,X509_ExtraData*extra,X509_Info*info)
  Read and validate a certificate chain. The arguments mostly work like
  x509_read_certificate, except that the info will automatically be reset
  (so you do not need to do that by yourself), and you must give it a
  chain rather than a single certificate.

void x509_reset_info(X509_Info*info)
  Resets the fields in the X509_Info structure (a null pointer is not
  allowed). All fields are reset to zero or null, and the output extra
  data object (if any) is also destroyed (the input is not destroyed,
  although it is replaced by a null pointer). The x509_read_chain function
  will automatically do this, but x509_read_certificate does not do this.


=== Certificate making functions ===

To create a new certificate, you must call the functions in the order:
  x509_new_certificate
  x509_set_serial (optional if option->set_serial is defined)
  x509_set_signature
  x509_add_extension (zero or more times)
  x509_finish_certificate

int x509_add_extension(X509_Encoder*enc,const uint8_t*oid,size_t oidlen,char crit,const ASN1_Value*value)
  Adds an extension to a certificate. You can call this any number of
  times, including zero times. You must call x509_set_signature before
  calling this function. This does not check for duplicate extensions;
  you must do that by yourself, and avoid calling it multiple times with
  the same OID, within one certificate.

  enc = The X509_Encoder for making the certificate.

  oid = The binary OID.

  oidlen = The length of the binary OID, in bytes.

  crit = Nonzero if critical or zero if noncritical.

  value = The value of this extension. (A copy will be made, so it is not
  necessary to keep it after this function returns.)

int x509_finish_certificate(X509_Encoder*enc)
  Finish the certificate. If successful, it will be written into the
  ASN1_Value structure specified by x509_new_certificate. The X509_Encoder
  object is not valid after this function returns.

X509_Encoder*x509_new_certificate(const X509_Info*info,const X509_Options*option,ASN1_Encoder*out)
  Starts creating a new certificate. Only version 3 certificates can be
  created by this function.

  info = Information to include in the new certificate. This must not be
  changed before the new certificate is finished.

  option = Options to create the new certificate (some parts are not used
  for creating certificates, and are only used for reading certificates).
  This must not be changed before the new certificate is finished.

  out = The ASN1_Encoder object that the new certificate will be written
  to. One item will be written to it, which is either the full certificate
  (including signature) or the main certificate, depending on the options.
  You should not use or free this before finishing the new certificate.

  Returns a X509_Encoder object, which is used with the other functions
  that are used to fill in the extensions and the other details.

int x509_set_serial(X509_Encoder*enc,const uint8_t*data,size_t len)
  Set the serial number of the certificate. The data is assumed to
  represent a non-negative big-endian integer; it will automatically
  convert it to canonical form (and add a leading zero byte if needed
  to make it positive).

  If this function is used, it must be used after x509_new_certificate but
  before any other functions with the same X509_Encoder object.

  This function will return X509_IMPROPER_VALUE if the serial number
  is zero, and it will not be set.

int x509_set_signature(X509_Encoder*enc,const ASN1_Value*alg,void*key)
  Set the signature algorithm and private key for the new certificate. This
  must be called before x509_add_extensions and x509_finish_certificate.

  enc = The X509_Encoder object which is being used to make a certificate.

  alg = The algorithm identifier. This can be either a sequence or a OID.
  If it is a OID, then it will make a sequence with that OID, and also NULL
  as well if it is a RSA algorithm.

  key = The private key. This library does not use the value, and is only
  used to pass to your own callback function. It is allowed to be null (if
  your callback function has some other way to determine the private key).
  If the X509_NO_SIGNATURE flag was specified, then the key is ignored.

int x509_sign_certificate(const X509_Info*info,const X509_Options*option,const ASN1_Value*tbs,void*privatekey,ASN1_Encoder*enc)
  Signs an existing unsigned certificate. (If the certificate is already
  signed, it will return X509_IMPROPER_TYPE.)

  info = Information about the certificate, which will be passed to the
  callback function in the X509_Options structure.

  option = Options for creating the certificate; the "make_signature"
  callback function in this structure is used.

  tbs = The certificate to be signed.

  privatekey = The private key. This library does not use the value, and is
  only used to pass to your own callback function. It is allowed to be null
  (if your callback function has some other way to know the private key).

  enc = The ASN1_Encoder object to write the signed certificate to.


=== Functions to set configurations ===

Use these functions to set the "in" field of the X509_Info structure,
before calling the other functions that use it. Calling these functions
is optional unless you require the functionality specified below. Note
that you must first call x509_extra_new or x509_extra_mirror.

int x509_set_needed_extended_key_usage(X509_ExtraData*ex,uint32_t usage,uint32_t flag)
  Set the expected extended key usage. (This function only supports the
  standard extended key usages; for other ones, you must define your own
  extension handler instead of using this one.) The usage should be a
  bitwise OR of the X509_EKU_* constants, and the flag should be a bitwise
  OR of the X509_EKUF_* constants.

  X509_EKUF_ONE_OF means that at least one of the key usages specified by
  the bits is required; it is OK if only some of them are present.

  X509_EKUF_ALL_OF means that all of the key usages specified by the bits
  are required.

  X509_EKUF_EXPLICIT means that 2.5.29.37.0 is not good enough, and that
  the OID for the expected extended key usage is required. If this flag is
  not specified, then 2.5.29.37.0 will always be acceptable.

int x509_set_needed_key_usage(X509_ExtraData*ex,uint16_t usage)
  Set the expected key usage. The usage should be a bitwise OR of the
  appropriate X509_KEYUSAGE_* constants. If zero, then it does not care
  about the key usage.

int x509_set_time(X509_ExtraData*ex,time_t now)
  Set the timestamp to use as the current time. This overrides the time set
  in the X509_Options structure.


=== Extra data functions ===

void x509_extra_delete(X509_ExtraData*extra,const void*key)
  Deletes the entry associated with the specified key. Does nothing if that
  key is not present, or if either argument is null. It will call the
  destructor function when deleting the entry.

void x509_extra_destroy(X509_ExtraData*extra)
  Destroys a X509_ExtraData; after using this, the pointer is not valid and
  should not be used. This will call the destructor functions for all data
  stored in this object.

void*x509_extra_destructor(X509_ExtraData*extra,const void*key)
  Retrieves a pointer to the destructor function for the specified key. If
  that key is not present, then it returns null.

void*x509_extra_find(X509_ExtraData*extra,const void*key,void(*destructor)(void*),const void*data,size_t size)
  Finds the data associated with the specified key, and returns a pointer
  to the data. If the key is not present, it will be added with the
  specified size and destructor and will copy the data given by the data
  pointer, or filled with zero if the data pointer is null. If the size
  and destructor are both zero then it will not add the key if it is not
  present and will return null; it also returns null if allocation fails.
  If the key is already present, then the third, fourth, and fifth
  arguments are ignored. The key is not allowed to be null.

X509_ExtraData*x509_extra_mirror(const X509_ExtraData*orig)
  Create a new empty X509_ExtraData which mirrors an existing one. The
  existing one should not be destroyed while the mirror exists. When
  trying to find data from the mirror, if it is not present in the mirror
  and the last three arguments are all zero or null, then it will look in
  the original as well. Destroying the mirror will not destroy the original
  and will not call any of the destructor functions in the original. If you
  call this function with null argument then it is same as x509_extra_new.

X509_ExtraData*x509_extra_new(void)
  Create a new X509_ExtraData, which is initially empty.


=== Trusting and revocation functions ===

(TODO)


=== Functions for keys and signature algorithms ===

int x509_check_signature(const X509_Info*info,const uint8_t*data,size_t len,const ASN1_Value*publickey,const ASN1_Value*algorithm,const ASN1_Value*signature)
  A function which can be used as the check_signature callback function
  and will use the algorithm list specified by x509_set_alglist.

  Returns X509_UNKNOWN_SIGNATURE if the pair of the algorithm OID and
  key OID is not in the list specified by x509_set_alglist.

int x509_make_signature(const X509_Info*info,const uint8_t*data,size_t len,void*privatekey,const ASN1_Value*algorithm,ASN1_Value*signature)
  A function which can be used as the make_signature callback function
  and will use the algorithm list specified by x509_set_alglist.

  Returns X509_UNKNOWN_SIGNATURE if the pair of the algorithm OID and
  key OID is not in the list specified by x509_set_alglist.

int x509_set_alglist(X509_ExtraData*info,X509_Options*option,X509_Algorithm*alglist,uint32_t algcount)
  Set the algorithm list to use.

  info = The extra data structure to use to set the algorithm list. If
  this is null, then it will set a global algorithm list, which will be
  used if this info is not set in the extra data structure.

  option = The X509_Options structure. If this is not null, then it will
  set the appropriate callback functions (check_signature and
  make_signature), but if null then it will skip this step (in case you
  will set them yourself, possibly to your own functions that will call
  the standard functions).

  alglist = The list of X509_Algorithm structures to use; this must be
  sorted in the correct order. This is like the extlist member of the
  X509_Options but for algorithms instead of extensions; it is sorted
  primarily by the signature OIDs and length, and secondarily by the key
  OIDs and length.

  algcount = The number of algorithm handlers.

void x509_sort_alglist(X509_Extension*alglist,uint32_t algcount)
  Sorts an algorithm list, like x509_sort_extlist but for the algorithm
  list instead of the extension list.


=== Miscellaneous functions ===

int x509_accept_any_self_signed(const X509_Info*info,const ASN1_Value*certificate)
  Ignores its argument and unconditionally returns X509_OK.

int x509_call_builtin_extension(const uint8_t*oid,size_t oidlen,X509_Info*info,const X509_Options*option,const ASN1_Value*data,uint8_t crit)
  Calls a built-in extension handler, for the specified OID. You can call
  this within your custom extension callback functions in order to do the
  built-in handling as well, in case you want to add your own logic before
  or after, or to do the built-in logic conditionally, etc. The return
  value of this function will be suitable to be returned by the callback
  function; note that it might be X509_DEFER_EXTENSION.

  oid = Binary OID.

  oidlen = Length (in bytes) of binary OID.

  info,option,data,crit = You should put the same values of the
  corresponding parameters that your callback function received. However,
  you can specify different values if you have a reason to do so.

void x509_sort_extlist(X509_Extension*extlist,uint32_t extcount)
  Sorts an extension list. The extension list must be sorted before it can
  be used, but if it is constant data then you can sort it by yourself (as
  described in the above documentation about the X509_Options structure),
  in which case you do not need to use this function.


=== Format of X.509 certificates ===

(See also RFC 5280, which describes many of these same things, although a
different profile (the Scorpion profile) is used here than the profile
described in that document (the RFC 5280 profile), although there are
(deliberately) many similarities, there are also some differences.)

The format of a X.509 certificate is a sequence with three fields (encoded
in DER format): the main certificate, the signature algorithm, and the
signature value.

The main certificate is a sequence with fields:

* Optional explicit type 0 integer which identifies the version number of
X.509. If omitted, it means version 1, 1 means version 2, and 2 means
version 3. Some fields require a specific version number; see below.

* The serial number, which is a positive integer. Each certificate that
an issuer issues is supposed to use a different serial number, and it is
recommended to limit this number to not more than twenty bytes. (Note
that the integer is positive and in canonical form iff either the first
byte is 0x00 and the second byte is in range 0x80 to 0xFF, or the first
byte is in range 0x01 to 0x7F.)

* The signature algorithm (which is the same as the signature algorithm
outside of the main certificate). This is a sequence with a OID and one
more optional field which can be any type according to the OID.

* The issuer name (see below). This is the same as the subject name of
the certificate that is issuing this one. If this is a self-signed
certificate, then the issuer name of this certificate is the same as the
subject name of this certificate.

* The validity date/time. This a sequence with two timestamps, the first
is the date at which the validity begins and the second is the expiry date.
These can be UTC time or Generalized time; if it is UTC time, then year
numbers in range 00 to 49 are treated as 2000 to 2049, and year numbers in
range 50 to 99 are treated as 1950 to 1999. If this is a certificate that
is not intended to expire, then you should specify 99991231235959Z (which
is Generalized time) for the expiry date.

* The subject name (see below). This is the name for whoever the
certificate has been issued to.

* The public key of this certificate. This is a sequence with two fields,
the first is the algorithm identifier (in the same format as the signature
algorithm identifier, as a sequence with one or two fields, but it is for
the key instead of for the signature), and the second is a bit string
(which usually contains the DER encoding of something; the number of unused
bits will be zero and the rest of the bytes are the DER encoding).

* An optional issuer ID, which a type 1 implicit bit string, and is only
allowed for a version 2 or version 3 certificate. This may be used together
with the issuer name in order to identify the issuer. It should match the
subject ID in the issuer certificate.

* An optional subject ID, which is a type 2 implicit bit string, and is
only allowed for a version 2 or version 3 certificate. This may be used
together with the subject name to identify the subject.

* If there are any extensions, an optional type 3 explicit sequence of
extensions. This is only allowed for a version 3 certificate, and should
have at least one extension if it is present.

The signature value is a bit string; the data being signed is the DER
representation of the main certificate.

A name is a sequence of sets of pieces; this sequence describes the
hierarchy of names by pieces. Each set of pieces (all at the same level
of the hierarchy) must have at least one piece, and each piece is a
sequence with two fields, being the OID of that piece and the value of
that piece (the value can be any type, but is usually some kind of
character strings). For the purpose of this profile, names must always
be treated as opaque data for the purpose of comparison, so that they
are compared according to the raw DER representation of the entire name;
however, decoding is possible if you want to display them or if you need
to decode a part of it to compare with a domain name or something else
which is specific to the application.

The following OIDs are used for the pieces of names:
  0.9.2342.19200300.100.1.25 = Domain name component
  1.2.840.113549.1.9.1 = Email address
  2.5.4.3 = Common name
  2.5.4.4 = Surname
  2.5.4.5 = Serial number
  2.5.4.6 = Country code
  2.5.4.7 = Locality
  2.5.4.8 = State of province
  2.5.4.9 = Street address
  2.5.4.10 = Organization
  2.5.4.11 = Organizational unit
  2.5.4.12 = Title
  2.5.4.13 = Description
  2.5.4.15 = Business category
  2.5.4.16 = Postal address
  2.5.4.17 = Postal code
  2.5.4.18 = Post office box
  2.5.4.20 = Telephone number
  2.5.4.41 = Name
  2.5.4.42 = Given name
  2.5.4.43 = Initials
  2.5.4.44 = Generation qualifier (e.g. "III", "Jr.")
  2.5.4.65 = Pseudonym
(You can also use OIDs that are not listed here, but these are a common
set of OIDs used for pieces of names.)

A country code is always a Printable string with two letters. Note that
these are not the same as the country codes for internet domain names.

RFC 5280 uses IA5 for the domain name components; this profile prefers
to use Printable string for domain name components.

RFC 5280 uses IA5 for email addresses; this profile prefers to use
Visible string for email addresses.

For most names that may be international, this profile prefers to use the
Graphic string type. However, for compatibility with RFC 5280, which does
not use the Graphic string type, any type may be used (and they must match
the same types in the issuer and subject certificates, so that the
comparison will work); however, if the common name field is used for a
domain name (which is not required if you are using the domain name
components or the subject alternate name extension; the recommended way to
represent domain names is the subject alternative names extension), then it
must not use the BMP string or Universal string types since those types are
not compatible with ASCII and cannot be decoded by a decoder that does not
expect those types (and Printable string is preferred for domain names, but
not for wildcard certificates).

Each extension is a sequence with two or three fields:

* The OID which identifies this extension. Duplicate OIDs are not allowed.

* True if this extension is critical or omitted if not critical. If the
extension is critical, then implementations should reject the certificate
if this extension cannot be used by this implementation.

* A octet string containing the DER encoding of a single item which is
the value of this extension.

Note that this specification does not use Unicode (unlike RFC 5280).
Software that conforms with this specification must treat Unicode text
as opaque data, and must use ASCII for URLs, domain names, etc. Most
pieces of names can use any character string type (including Unicode
types), but ISO 2022 is preferred.

Some of the common (or otherwise important) standard extensions are
listed in the below section. See also the Scorpion protocol specification
for more extensions that are not listed in this file.


=== Standard extensions ===

2.5.29.9
  This extension is used to specify attributes that are not a part of the
  name. The value is a sequence of attributes, where each attribute is a
  sequence with two fields, the first field being a OID and the second
  field being a set of the values of the attribute (what the values are
  expected to be is defined by the OID).

  Many of the same OIDs can be used that are used for names.

  This extension is supposed to be non-critical.

2.5.29.15
  This extension identifies the usage of the key in this certificate. If
  this extension is present, then the certificate is not valid for using
  the key for the purposes not listed in this extension. The value of
  this extension is a bit string, where the bits are defined as follows:

  (0) This key is used for verifying digital signatures other than the
  signature of the certificate and of revocation lists.

  (1) This key is used for non-repudiation to protect against the signing
  entity falsely denying some action.

  (2) This key used for enciphering private or secret keys, for key
  transport.

  (3) This key is used for directly encrypting data, rather than using an
  intermediate symmetric cipher (which would use (2) instead). This is
  not common.

  (4) This key is used for key agreement.

  (5) This key is used for the signatures of certificates. See also the
  basic constraints extension, which also describes this use.

  (6) This key is used for signatures of revocation lists.

  (7) Encipher only (if (4) is also specified)

  (8) Decipher only (if (4) is also specified)

2.5.29.17
  This is the "subject alternative name" extension. The value is a
  sequence of alternative names, where each alternative name is one
  of the following implicit typed values:

  [0] A implicit sequence of a object identifier followed by a explicit
  type 0 value of any type according to the specified OID.

  [1] Email address.

  [2] Domain name. If it starts with *. then this certificate is only
  valid for all subdomains (one level) of the specified domain name.

  [3] X.400 address.

  [4] A name in the same format as the subject name and issuer name of
  the certificate.

  [5] EDI party (I don't know what this means).

  [6] Absolute URL.

  [7] IP address. This is a octet string, in network byte order, with
  four bytes for version 4 or sixteen bytes for version 6.

  [8] Object identifier.

  The formats that directly use text strings are encoded as ASCII.

  If this extension is present and is critical, then the name in the main
  part of the certificate may be empty, but any certificates that this one
  issues should include the issuer alternative name extension.

2.5.29.18
  This is the "issuer alternative name" extension, which has the same
  format as the subject alternative name extension, but it is the
  alternative names of the issuer instead of the subject.

  It is allowed (and is recommended) to ignore this extension when
  validating certificates; the issuer name and issuer ID are used instead.

2.5.29.19
  This is basic constraints, which is used to specifying how many more
  certificates in the chain may be issued starting with this one.

  The value is a sequence. If this certificate cannot issue any other
  certificates, then this sequence is empty. If it can issue another
  certificate, then the first field is true, and the second field is
  omitted if there is no further constraint or a non-negative integer
  of the maximum number of intermediate certificates between this one
  and the end certificate (so zero means it can only issue certificates
  that cannot themself issue certificates).

  This extension must be critical for certificates that issue other
  certificates; for other certifiates it may be critical.

  If extension 2.5.29.15 is present as well as this one, then the
  sequence in this extension is empty if and only if the bit (5) is
  not set in the 2.5.29.15 extension.

2.5.29.21
  This is only used in a revocation list and it means that the certificate
  is revoked and indicates the reason, which is enumerated and is one of:

  (0) It is revoked but none of the below reasons apply, or the reason is
  unknown, or unspecified for some other reason.

  (1) The subject's private key is compromised.

  (2) The certificate authority's private key is compromised.

  (3) Nothing is compromised, but the name, affiliation, or other
  information in the certificate is no longer valid, because their name,
  affiliation, etc has been changed.

  (4) Nothing is compromised, but the certificate has been superseded, so
  this version of the certificate is no longer valid.

  (5) The certificate is no longer needed, or the subject no longer owns
  and controls the domain name in the certificate.

  (6) The certificate is temporarily revoked. In future, it might become
  permanently revoked or unrevoked.

  (8) The certificate is not revoked. It is either valid or it has expired
  or is invalid for some reason other than having been revoked. This number
  is intended to be used in case it has previously been temporarily revoked
  but is no longer revoked.

  (9) The privileges granted by the certificate have been revoked.

  (10) This number is only relevant for attribute authorities, which are
  not used by the specification in this document.

  (11) The key or algorithm is weak, or the way that the key was generated
  is considered to have been unsafe. (Not specified in RFC 5280.)

  Note that number 7 is not used.

2.5.29.30
  This extension defines name constraints for any certificate that
  this one issues and that any further certificates in the chain issue
  (but not to this certificate). Restrictions apply to the subject name
  and alternative names.

  The value of this extension is a sequence with two fields, both optional.
  The first field is a implicit type 0 sequence of permitted subtrees, and
  the second field is a implicit type 1 sequence of excluded subtrees.

  Each subtree specification is a sequence with up to three fields. The
  first field is the same format as a single alternative name. The other
  two are used for minimum and maximum distances, but those are not
  applicable to the standard constraints described here.

  If a name appears in the excluded subtrees, then that name is not allowed
  in the name or alternative names of the certificates it issues, even if
  the permitted subtrees allows it.

  [1] It can be a full email address or only the domain name. If it is only
  the domain name, then it matches any email address with that domain name.
  If it is a domain name preceded by a dot, then it matches any email
  address with any subdomains of that domain name, but does not match any
  email address with that domain name itself. This matches both the subject
  alternative name and any pieces of the subject name that use the OID
  1.2.840.113549.1.9.1.

  [2] The constraint is a domain name, and matches that domain name as well
  as any subdomains. According to this specification, when the certificate
  is checked for validating the domain name, it should use this constraint
  to check if the domain name it is using is acceptable according to this
  certificate chain, regardless of where it gets the domain name from
  (usually it comes from a URL, rather than from the certificate, although
  it would also check that the domain name in the end certificate's subject
  alternative name (or subject name, if the SAN is absent or does not have
  the domain name) matches).

  [6] The constraint is a domain name, which matches any URL with that
  domain name, or a domain name preceded by a dot, which matches any URL
  that has any subdomain of that domain name, but does not match the domain
  name itself. This constraint does not apply to the rest of the URL.

  [7] The length is twice as long as it is in the alternative name
  extension; it starts with the value, and then the mask follows. It
  matches any IP address that, when you AND by the mask, will produce
  the value of this constraint.

2.5.29.37
  This extension identifies what the certificate can be used for; see also
  2.5.29.15. This is a sequence of one or more OIDs that identify the uses
  of the certificate. Some possible uses are:

  2.5.29.37.0 = Any usage is possible, although they are not specified
  explicitly. This is intended for the possibility that an application
  expects other usages to be listed here but that the certificate is not
  exclusively for such purposes and can also be used for other purposes.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.1 = TLS server authentication.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.2 = TLS client authentication.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.3 = It can be used for signing executable programs.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.4 = It is used for email.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.8 = It can be used for binding the hash of a file to
  a timestamp.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.9 = Signing OCSP responses.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.21 = SSH client authentication.

  1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.22 = SSH server authentication.

  (Other uses are possible; you can make up your own OIDs for your own
  uses if necessary.)

